<p>Most property insurance claims in Central Texas eventually involve the roof. Hail, straight-line wind, falling limbs, and the occasional ice storm all take a toll, and roof damage insurance claims in San Marcos often turn on details a homeowner never sees: which line items made it into the estimate, how depreciation was calculated, and whether the policy pays replacement cost at all. As a <a href="/">public adjuster in San Marcos</a>, we focus on those details so the settlement reflects the roof you actually have.</p>

<h2>Roof Types We See Across Hays County</h2>
<p>Local construction varies a lot within a short drive, and each roof type raises different claim questions.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Composition shingle roofs</strong> dominate the newer subdivisions along I-35 in San Marcos, Kyle, and Buda. Disputes usually involve whether damage is functional, how many slopes are affected, and whether repairs can match the existing shingles.</li>
<li><strong>Standing-seam and exposed-fastener metal roofs</strong> are common on Hill Country homes, barns, and outbuildings toward Wimberley and Dripping Springs. Cosmetic damage endorsements and fastener damage are frequent issues.</li>
<li><strong>Flat and low-slope commercial roofs</strong> along the I-35 frontage involve membranes, insulation boards, and rooftop equipment, which call for a different kind of scope. Our <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a> page covers those.</li>
<li><strong>Older roofs in historic neighborhoods</strong> near downtown may have multiple layers, unusual materials, or decking that does not meet current spacing, all of which affect cost.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What We Check in a Roof Insurance Estimate</h2>
<p>A roof estimate is a list of materials and labor. When an item is left off, it simply is not paid. We build our own line-item estimate and compare it against the insurer&rsquo;s, looking for gaps like these:</p>
<table>
