<h2>Local Codes and How They Affect a Roof Claim</h2>
<p>The City of San Marcos has enforced the 2021 International Codes, including the IRC and IBC, since April 1, 2023, under Ordinance 2023-09. One local amendment is directly relevant to roofs: a roof covering replacement permit is not required unless the replacement roof is heavier than the existing roof covering. Check the city&rsquo;s <a href="https://www.sanmarcostx.gov/1289/Codes-Ordinances" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Codes &amp; Ordinances page</a> for the current rules.</p>
<p>Code requirements still apply to the work even when no permit is pulled, and they can add cost. Whether the insurer pays for that added cost depends on your ordinance or law coverage, which is often a separate, limited coverage. Outside city limits, Hays County does not issue residential building permits, and nearby cities run their own permit offices. Our <a href="/building-codes-insurance-repairs-san-marcos/">building codes and insurance repairs</a> article explains the interaction in more depth.</p>

<h2>How Your Policy Pays for a Roof</h2>
<p>Two roofs with identical damage can be paid very differently depending on the policy:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Replacement cost value (RCV):</strong> the insurer usually pays actual cash value first, then releases recoverable depreciation after repairs are complete.</li>
<li><strong>Actual cash value (ACV) only:</strong> depreciation is not recovered, so the age and condition of the roof weigh heavily.</li>
<li><strong>Roof payment schedules:</strong> some Texas policies pay a percentage of roof cost based on the roof&rsquo;s age.</li>
<li><strong>Wind/hail deductibles:</strong> often a percentage of the dwelling limit rather than a flat amount.</li>
</ul>
<p>Read the declarations page and roof-related endorsements before the inspection. Our <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">RCV versus ACV guide</a> breaks the math down with examples.</p>

<h2>Before the Insurance Adjuster Inspects Your Roof</h2>
<p>The insurer&rsquo;s inspection often sets the tone for the rest of the claim, so a little preparation goes a long way:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Photograph from the ground first.</strong> Take wide shots of every side of the house, then closer shots of gutters, downspouts, vents, and anything on the ground such as shingle pieces or granules collected at downspout outlets.</li>
<li><strong>Check the attic and ceilings.</strong> Look for daylight through the decking, damp insulation, or new stains, and photograph them with the date.</li>
<li><strong>Protect the roof, carefully.</strong> If water is coming in, a contractor can tarp the damaged area. Keep the invoice; reasonable emergency repairs are usually part of the claim.</li>
<li><strong>Collect the paperwork.</strong> Have the declarations page, roof endorsements, any prior roof invoices, and the roof&rsquo;s installation date ready.</li>
<li><strong>Be present if you can.</strong> Walk the adjuster to the interior damage and ask which slopes and items they are including.</li>
</ul>
<p>Avoid getting on a steep or wet roof yourself. A fall is far more costly than any claim. Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> lists what to gather, and the <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup</a> helps confirm the date when weather caused the damage.</p>

<h2>Our Roof Claim Process</h2>
<ol>
<li>Review the policy, declarations page, and any correspondence from the insurer.</li>
<li>Inspect the roof slope by slope, plus the attic and interior where leaks appeared.</li>
<li>Document damage with organized photos and measurements, and confirm the storm date when weather caused the loss.</li>
<li>Prepare an Xactimate estimate and present it to the carrier with a clear explanation of each difference.</li>
<li>Track depreciation release, supplements, and final payment until the claim is closed.</li>
</ol>

<h2>When a Roof Claim Needs Outside Help</h2>
<p>Consider talking with a San Marcos public adjuster if the insurer approved a repair but your roofer says replacement is needed, if the estimate omits items your contractor priced, if depreciation looks excessive for the roof&rsquo;s age, or if a leak claim was denied as wear and tear. Roof damage from hail and wind often overlaps, so our <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail damage claims</a> and <a href="/services/wind-damage-claims/">wind damage claims</a> pages may also help. When you are ready, request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">free claim review</a>.</p>
